6) Real-time streams (Socket.IO)

CoinDCX’s help pages state Socket.IO is used; futures docs and your sample show a unified event shape:

{
  "ts": 1714653301197,         // event timestamp (ms)
  "vs": 10037615,              // version / sequence for book
  "asks": { "0.10828":"260", ... },
  "bids": { "0.10758":"38692.1224", ... },
  "E": 1714653301194,          // event time (book-specific)
  "pr": "spot",                // 'spot' or 'futures'
  "s": "BTCUSDT"               // symbol
}


Treat each message as a partial depth update keyed by stringified price to string quantity (0 means remove).

Maintain per-pair monotonic vs (version) to apply updates in order and detect gaps; if a gap is detected, re-snapshot via REST orderbook and replay only newer deltas. (Fields and book shape consistent with futures order book docs.) 
CoinDCX

Heartbeat
Futures docs explicitly require a heartbeat check per channel; implement ping/pong or a timer that resubscribes/reconnects if no data within a tight SLA (e.g., 2–3s). 
CoinDCX

Note: CoinDCX’s public docs don’t publish a canonical WS URL on the help pages. Since you’re already receiving messages, keep your internal WS URL in infra config. Use this section’s schema and sequencing logic to implement the consumer. (If needed, REST endpoints above provide fallback/bootstrapping.) 
